No markdown library installed. Only @gravity-ui/uikit, react, react-dom. I'll write a small markdown parser that outputs React elements (safe, no dangerouslySetInnerHTML).

Plan:
- `src/markdown.tsx` — parser: block-level (headings, code fences, blockquote, lists, hr, paragraphs) + inline (bold, italic, strikethrough, inline code, links, hard line breaks).
- `src/App.tsx` — Gravity UI form: Card with header, TextArea for input, live preview rendered side-by-side (and maybe a toggle for side-by-side/preview-only). Use Gravity components: TextArea, Text, Card, SegmentedRadioGroup maybe. Keep it reasonably simple but polished.

Note: package name "cross-markdown-editor". The task: "Добавь в форму поле, где пользователь пишет текст с markdown-разметкой и сразу видит форматирование." — Add a field to the form where the user writes markdown and immediately sees formatting. There's no existing form, so I create the form with this field.

Implement the markdown parser:

```tsx
import React from 'react';

// inline parsing
function renderInline(text: string, keyPrefix: string): React.ReactNode[] { ... }
```

Inline rules with regex: code `...`, bold **...**, italic *...* or _..._, strikethrough ~~...~~, links [text](url), images maybe skip. Escape handling not needed deeply.

Approach: tokenize inline with a combined regex, recursively render content of bold/italic.

Block parsing: split source into lines, iterate:
- ``` fence start → accumulate until closing fence → <pre><code>
- heading ^#{1,6}\s
- hr ^\s*(-{3,}|\*{3,}|_{3,})\s*$
- blockquote ^>\s? consecutive lines → nested render blocks
- unordered list ^\s*[-*+]\s+ consecutive
- ordered list ^\s*\d+[.)]\s+ consecutive
- else paragraph: accumulate until blank line; join with \n → soft line breaks as <br/>.

Render with keys.

App layout: 
- Container with max width, a Card containing form: Text (h2) title, a TextArea with label "Текст с Markdown-разметкой", and a preview panel labeled "Предпросмотр" that updates on change. Side-by-side on wide screens using CSS grid.

Gravity UI components: `TextArea`, `Text`, `Card`, `Button` maybe. TextArea supports `rows`. Use `useState` for value with some initial demo markdown so the preview shows immediately.

Styling: inline styles or a CSS file. I'll add a small `src/App.css`? Vite supports importing css. Keep inline styles to avoid extra config — fine.

Let me write markdown.tsx carefully.

Edge cases: avoid ReDoS; keep regexes simple.

Inline tokenizer regex:
```
/(\[([^\]]*)\]\(([^)\s]+)\))|(`([^`]+)`)|(\*\*([^*]+)\*\*)|(\*([^*]+)\*)|(__([^_]+)__)|(_([^_]+)_)|(~~([^~]+)~~)/g
```
Order matters: check code first, then bold before italic. With a single combined regex and alternation order, leftmost match wins; at same position, earlier alternative wins. Code first is good.

Process: iterate matches, push plain text between matches, push element for match.

Links: sanitize href — only allow http(s):, mailto:, or relative (#, /). Otherwise render as text. 

Let me write the code.

```tsx
// src/markdown.tsx
import React from 'react';

const INLINE_RE = /(`([^`]+)`)|(\*\*([^*]+)\*\*)|(\*([^*]+)\*)|(__([^_]+)__)|(_([^_]+)_)|(~~([^~]+)~~)|(\[([^\]]+)\]\(([^)\s]+)(?:\s+"([^"]*)")?\))/g;
```

Hmm, links should come before italic maybe? `[text](url)` contains no asterisks typically; order code → link → bold → italic → strike. Fine.

Actually a simpler approach: process with a single regex, use match groups. Let me write a function `renderInline(text: string): React.ReactNode[]`.

```tsx
function isSafeUrl(url: string): boolean {
  return /^(https?:\/\/|mailto:|#|\/)/i.test(url);
}

export function renderInline(text: string): React.ReactNode[] {
  const nodes: React.ReactNode[] = [];
  const re = /(`[^`]+`)|(\*\*[^*]+\*\*)|(\*[^*]+\*)|(__[^_]+__)|(_[^_]+_)|(~~[^~]+~~)|(\[[^\]]+\]\([^)\s]+\))/g;
  let last = 0;
  let m: RegExpExecArray | null;
  let k = 0;
  while ((m = re.exec(text)) !== null) {
    if (m.index > last) nodes.push(text.slice(last, m.index));
    const tok = m[0];
